  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Community Grievance Management and Resilience Systems in the Middle Belt - IFIT</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Facilitated Negotiations Between the Bassa and Egbura Peoples of Nasarawa State-Phase 3.
The Bassa and Egbura ethnic groups have violently clashed over land ownership, 
chiefdoms, and political power since the precolonial era. The conflict has intensified in 
recent years leading to the formation of ethnic-based militia groups and large-scale 
displacements of the Bassa ethnic group out of Toto Local Government Area (LGA) and 
into Nasarawa LGA and the Federal Capital Territory in 2018. The displacements have 
weakened traditional and public authority in some of these communities, allowing the radical 
Islamic sect Darussalam to recruit members and set up camps, significantly increasing 
lawlessness, kidnapping, and rural banditry. Key leaders of the ethnic groups formally requested the support of the Institute for Integrated Transitions (IFIT) and its Middle Belt Brain Trust (MBBT) to facilitate a negotiation process that would enable the Bassa people to return to their original locations. The envisaged 
agreement would aim to settle existing intra ethnic divides, overcome disputes about land 
and chiefdoms, and create new mechanisms to settle future disputes. 
This agreement came to fruition with the FCDO&#x2019;s support through which IFIT carried out a 
two phase facilitated negotiation between the Bassa and Egbura people. So far, the 
agreement has allowed 31,000 persons from over 50 communities to return home. 
Additionally, the agreement launched the formation of a joint grievance management 
forum to address emerging conflicts.</narrative>
  </description>
